### How do you build your own HashMap?

Building a HashMap from scratch involves four components: a Node class to hold key-value pairs, a bucket array, a hash function, and resize logic. The implementation below is a simplified version that demonstrates the core mechanics.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Objects;

public class BankHashMap<K, V> {

    private static class Node<K, V> {
        final int hash;
        final K key;
        V value;
        Node<K, V> next;

        Node(int hash, K key, V value, Node<K, V> next) {
            this.hash = hash;
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
            this.next = next;
        }
    }

    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 16;
    private static final float LOAD_FACTOR = 0.75f;

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    private Node<K, V>[] table = new Node[DEFAULT_CAPACITY];
    private int size = 0;

    // Spread function: mix high bits into low bits for better distribution
    private int hash(Object key) {
        if (key == null) return 0;
        int h = key.hashCode();
        return h ^ (h >>> 16);
    }

    // Bucket index from hash
    private int indexFor(int hash, int capacity) {
        return hash & (capacity - 1);
    }

    public V put(K key, V value) {
        int h = hash(key);
        int index = indexFor(h, table.length);

        // Walk the chain: if key exists, replace value
        for (Node<K, V> node = table[index]; node != null; node = node.next) {
            if (node.hash == h && Objects.equals(node.key, key)) {
                V oldValue = node.value;
                node.value = value;
                return oldValue;
            }
        }

        // Key not found: prepend new node
        table[index] = new Node<>(h, key, value, table[index]);
        size++;

        if (size > table.length * LOAD_FACTOR) {
            resize();
        }
        return null;
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        int h = hash(key);
        int index = indexFor(h, table.length);

        for (Node<K, V> node = table[index]; node != null; node = node.next) {
            if (node.hash == h && Objects.equals(node.key, key)) {
                return node.value;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public V remove(K key) {
        int h = hash(key);
        int index = indexFor(h, table.length);
        Node<K, V> prev = null;

        for (Node<K, V> node = table[index]; node != null; prev = node, node = node.next) {
            if (node.hash == h && Objects.equals(node.key, key)) {
                if (prev == null) {
                    table[index] = node.next;
                } else {
                    prev.next = node.next;
                }
                size--;
                return node.value;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    private void resize() {
        int newCapacity = table.length * 2;
        Node<K, V>[] newTable = new Node[newCapacity];

        for (Node<K, V> head : table) {
            for (Node<K, V> node = head; node != null; ) {
                Node<K, V> next = node.next;
                int newIndex = indexFor(node.hash, newCapacity);
                node.next = newTable[newIndex];
                newTable[newIndex] = node;
                node = next;
            }
        }
        table = newTable;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean containsKey(K key) {
        return get(key) != null;
    }
}
```

### How do you test this implementation?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.core.BankHashMap;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BankHashMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankHashMap<String, BigDecimal> balances = new BankHashMap<>();

        // put operations
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("500000.00"));
        balances.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("1200000.00"));
        balances.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("2500000.00"));

        // get operations
        System.out.println("SAV2001 balance: " + balances.get("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("CUR3001 balance: " + balances.get("CUR3001"));
        System.out.println("Unknown account: " + balances.get("ACC-999"));
        System.out.println("Size: " + balances.size());

        // replace existing key
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("550000.00"));
        System.out.println("Updated SAV2001: " + balances.get("SAV2001"));

        // remove
        balances.remove("FD4001");
        System.out.println("After remove FD4001, size: " + balances.size());
        System.out.println("FD4001: " + balances.get("FD4001"));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001 balance: 500000.00
CUR3001 balance: 1200000.00
Unknown account: null
Size: 3
Updated SAV2001: 550000.00
After remove FD4001, size: 2
FD4001: null
```

### How does the hash function work?

The hash function has two steps. First, it calls `key.hashCode()` to get the raw hash. Second, it applies a spread function that XORs the hash with its own upper 16 bits shifted right. This mixing ensures that differences in the upper bits also influence the lower bits, which is important because the bucket index uses only the lower bits (via `hash & (capacity - 1)`).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class HashFunctionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String key = "SAV2001";
        int h = key.hashCode();
        int spread = h ^ (h >>> 16);
        int capacity = 16;
        int bucket = spread & (capacity - 1);

        System.out.println("Key: " + key);
        System.out.println("hashCode(): " + h);
        System.out.println("After spread: " + spread);
        System.out.println("Bucket index (capacity=" + capacity + "): " + bucket);
    }
}
```

```text
Key: SAV2001
hashCode(): -1808287924
After spread: -1808223900
Bucket index (capacity=16): 4
```

### How does resizing work?

Resizing happens when the number of entries exceeds `capacity * loadFactor`. The default load factor is 0.75, meaning a 16-slot array resizes when it holds 13 entries.

During resize, a new array of double capacity is created. Every existing entry is rehashed into the new array because the bucket index depends on the capacity: `hash & (newCapacity - 1)` produces different indices than `hash & (oldCapacity - 1)`.

- Initial capacity: 16. Resize threshold: 12.
- After first resize: capacity 32. Threshold: 24.
- After second resize: capacity 64. Threshold: 48.

Resize is O(n) because every entry must be moved. However, it happens infrequently enough that the amortised cost of put remains O(1).

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Not checking equals() after hash match

```java
// WRONG: comparing only hash codes
for (Node<K, V> node = table[index]; node != null; node = node.next) {
    if (node.hash == h) { // hash collision! Different keys can have same hash!
        return node.value;
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: check hash AND equals
for (Node<K, V> node = table[index]; node != null; node = node.next) {
    if (node.hash == h && Objects.equals(node.key, key)) {
        return node.value;
    }
}
```

#### Mistake: Using modulo instead of bitwise AND

```java
// WRONG: modulo can return negative index
int index = hash % capacity; // negative if hash is negative!
```

```java
// CORRECT: bitwise AND with (capacity - 1), requires power-of-2 capacity
int index = hash & (capacity - 1); // always non-negative
```

#### Mistake: Forgetting to rehash during resize

```java
// WRONG: copying entries to same index in new array
for (int i = 0; i < oldTable.length; i++) {
    newTable[i] = oldTable[i]; // WRONG! Index depends on capacity!
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: recompute index for each entry
for (Node<K, V> head : oldTable) {
    for (Node<K, V> node = head; node != null; ) {
        Node<K, V> next = node.next;
        int newIndex = node.hash & (newCapacity - 1);
        node.next = newTable[newIndex];
        newTable[newIndex] = node;
        node = next;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Java 8 treeification.** OpenJDK's HashMap converts a chain to a red-black tree when the chain length reaches 8 (TREEIFY_THRESHOLD) and the table has at least 64 slots. This changes worst-case get from O(n) to O(log n).
- **Power-of-two capacity.** The capacity is always a power of 2 so that `hash & (capacity - 1)` works as a fast modulo. Java's HashMap enforces this by rounding up the initial capacity.
- **Null key handling.** Java's HashMap stores the null key at index 0 with a hash of 0.
- **Thread safety.** A custom HashMap is not thread-safe. For concurrent access, use ConcurrentHashMap or synchronise externally.
- **Open addressing alternative.** Instead of chaining, open addressing (linear probing, quadratic probing) stores entries directly in the array. IdentityHashMap uses this approach. It has better cache locality but worse performance under high load factors.

### How does this compare to Java's real HashMap?

| Feature | Our BankHashMap | Java HashMap (JDK 17+) |
|---|---|---|
| Collision handling | Chaining (linked list) | Chaining + tree (at length 8) |
| Spread function | h ^ (h >>> 16) | Same |
| Load factor | 0.75 | 0.75 (configurable) |
| Null key | Not handled | Stored at index 0 |
| Iteration | Not implemented | Via EntrySet, KeySet, Values |
| Fail-fast | No | Yes (modCount check) |
| Thread safety | None | None (use ConcurrentHashMap) |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the time complexity of put and get?" : O(1) amortised, O(n) worst case (all keys in one bucket). With treeification (Java 8+), worst case is O(log n).
- "What triggers a resize?" : When size exceeds capacity times load factor (default 0.75).
- "Why is capacity a power of 2?" : So that `hash & (capacity - 1)` is equivalent to `hash % capacity` but faster.
- "How does the spread function help?" : It mixes high bits into low bits to reduce clustering when the table is small.
- "What happens if two keys have the same hash?" : They land in the same bucket and form a chain. get() walks the chain and uses equals() to find the correct key.
